th-grade public school students in 2022 with those from 2019, the previous assessment year, \\n0 scored higher; 43 scored lower; and 10 had no significant change in scores. \\nResults for states/jurisdictions reflect the performance of students in public schools only and are reported along with the results for public school students in the nation.  Explore the interactive map below to see which states/jurisdictions saw increases or decreases and by how much. Scroll down to explore the magnitude of average score changes across states/jurisdictions. Select focal year2022Select comparison year2019Figure Change in average scores between 2019 and 2022 for fourth-grade public school students assessed in NAEP mathematics, by state/jurisdictionBetween 2019 and 20220states/jurisdictions0states/jurisdictions0states/jurisdictions0states/jurisdictionshad a score increasehad no significant change in scorehad a score decreasehad no data or not applicableNOTE: DS = Department of Defense Education Activity (DoDEA), a federally operated nonpublic school system responsible for educating children of military families. See more about DoDEA.The table below shows the number of states/jurisdictions with score increases or decreases in 2022 and how many states/jurisdictions scored higher, lower, or not significantly different than the nation (public) in NAEP mathematics.Number of states/jurisdictions in 2022Change in average NAEP mathematics score between 2019 and 2022TotalHigher than nation (public)Not significantly different from nation (public)Lower than nation (public)Score increase0000No significant change in score10442Score decrease43131713To compare average mathematics scores between states/jurisdictions and the nation (public), use the NAEP State Profiles Tool.Share this result:Size of State Score ChangesExamine the magnitude of score changesIn the 43 states/jurisdictions where scores were lower in 2022 than 2019, the size of the score differences ranged from 2 to 14 points. The av